course: dictionary
lesson: 14968
title: Remit: the job you are given
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A remit is the area of work or responsibility given to a person or group. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in official letters, school rules and news reports. «Cleaning the playground is outside my remit.» The speaker means that cleaning is not their job, so they are not responsible for it.
explain: Read the words around remit to find its meaning. «The committee's remit is to plan the school fair.» The committee is in charge of the fair, so its remit is the fair. A good check is to swap your meaning in: "The committee's job is to plan the school fair" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Remit can also be a verb in formal language. In business, «Please remit payment within thirty days» means «Please pay within thirty days», because to remit is to send money as a payment. In law, a judge can remit a punishment, which means cancel it, or remit a case, which means send it back to another court.
fact: Remit (noun) means the area of work or responsibility a person or group is given.
fact: «Outside my remit» means not my job to deal with.
fact: To remit (verb, formal) means to send money as a payment.
fact: To remit can also mean to cancel a debt or a punishment, or to send a case back to another court.
fact: A remittance is money sent to someone far away, such as money sent home.
exercise: «Cleaning the playground is outside my remit.» What is a remit? | A kind of playground swing | A school trip to the zoo | *The work a person is responsible for
exercise: «Please remit payment within thirty days.» What does remit mean here? | To ring the bell | *To send the money that is owed | To write a poem about money | To tear up the bill
exercise: Look at «The committee's remit is to plan the school fair.» What is the committee in charge of? | Closing the school for the day | *Planning the school fair | Painting the hall | Eating cake at the fair
test: «The judge chose to remit the fine.» What does remit mean here? | To double the punishment | To write a new law | *To cancel the punishment | To go to bed early
test: «Mum's brother sent a remittance home to his family.» What is a remittance? | A small red bag | *Money sent to someone far away | A type of train ticket | A polite letter of thanks
exam: Ms Patel said, «The judge decided to remit the case to another court.» What does remit mean here? | To give the case a prize | To make the case into a poem | To lock the case in a cupboard | *To send the case back to another court
